Metadata: 
Patient Name: Margaret 
Age: 74 
Language Profile: Early-stage Alzheimer's Disease 
Session ID: 001 
Time: 10:45 AM 

*CLI*: Good morning, Margaret. Welcome to our session today. How are you feeling?

(Pause: ..)

*Margaret*: (..) Oh, good... morning. I think. *pauses* Um, what was the question again?

*CLI*: That's okay, let's start fresh. Can you tell me about your living situation? Where do you live?

*Margaret*: I-I live in Willowbrook Apartments. Or at least, I try to. *pauses* You know, it's not easy keeping track of things sometimes.

*CLI*: It can be challenging. Have you noticed any changes in your daily routines or habits recently?

*Margaret*: Changes? (pauses) Um... I think I left the stove on last night. Or was it this morning? *laughs nervously*

*CLI*: Let's try to focus on one thing at a time. Can you show me where you usually keep your keys?

*Margaret*: Keys... (pauses) Oh, um, they're around here somewhere. I think. *looks around*

*CLI*: Okay, that's okay. It can be frustrating when we can't find things. Have you noticed any memory loss or difficulties with learning new information recently?

*Margaret*: Memory? (pauses) Um... well, I'm trying to remember where I put my... uh- -my... *trails off*

*CLI*: That's okay, Margaret. It's not uncommon for people with early-stage Alzheimer's to experience memory lapses. Let's try something else. Can you tell me about your favorite hobby or activity?

*Margaret*: (pauses) I-I used to love painting. I used to make beautiful... *pauses* Um, what was it called again? *laughs*

*CLI*: It's okay, Margaret. We can figure it out together. Maybe we can find some old art supplies and you can try painting again?

*Margaret*: (smiling) That sounds lovely. Thank you, Dr. Chen.

(Pause: ..)

